    "knowledge_point": "Comparing Factored Forms",
    "problem": "The equation\n\n$$\nt(2{x}^{2}-3x+1)=-6{x}^{2}+9x-3\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $t$ is a constant. What is the value of $t?$",
    "worked_solution": "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means that after distributing $t$ on the left-hand side, the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nt(2{x}^{2}-3x+1)=2t{x}^{2}-3tx+t\n$$\n\nThis must equal the right-hand side. So, we have\n\n$$\n2t{x}^{2}-3tx+t=-6{x}^{2}+9x-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the coefficients of corresponding terms. \n\nComparing the ${x}^{2}$ terms, we have\n\n$$\n2t=-6\\,⇒\\,t=-3.\n$$\n\nLet's verify that value $t=-3$ produces the correct linear coefficient and constant term:\n\n- $-3t=(-3)(-3)=9\\,✓$\n\n- $t=-3\\,✓$\n\nSo, our solution is valid.",
